{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "Identify: finding a %, increase/decrease, % change, or reverse %",
   "Convert the percentage to a multiplier (e.g. 35% → 0.35, or 1.35 for increase)",
   "Multiply (or divide for reverse percentages)",
   "For % change: (new − original) ÷ original × 100"
  ],
  "title": "How to Work with Percentages",
  "content": "<p>A <strong>percentage</strong> means 'out of 100'. To find a percentage of an amount, convert the percentage to a decimal (divide by 100) and multiply.</p><p><strong>Percentage increase/decrease:</strong> Use a <strong>multiplier</strong>. For an increase of \\(r\\%\\), multiply by \\(1 + \\frac{r}{100}\\). For a decrease, multiply by \\(1 - \\frac{r}{100}\\). Example: 15% increase → multiply by 1.15; 20% decrease → multiply by 0.80.</p><p><strong>Percentage change:</strong> \\(\\frac{\\text{change}}{\\text{original}} \\times 100\\). Always divide by the <em>original</em> value.</p><p><strong>Reverse percentages:</strong> If you know the value <em>after</em> a percentage change, divide by the multiplier to find the original. For example, if a price after a 20% increase is £60, the original is \\(60 \\div 1.2 = £50\\).</p>",
  "example": "<p><strong>Find</strong> 15% of £240</p><p><strong>Step 1:</strong> 15% = 0.15</p><p><strong>Step 2:</strong> \\(240 \\times 0.15 = 36\\)</p><p><strong>Answer:</strong> £36</p>"
 },
